  counter, and then two more people from
  the café, still in their aprons.

  By the time they reached the Narrows
  landing, word had gotten ahead of
  them. People phone each other on an
  island. Thirty or forty of them stood
  along the seawall, and somebody had
  brought a handbell from the primary
  school, and somebody else had a
  saucepan and a wooden spoon. When Walt
  sounded the signal, the whole seawall
  answered with a racket of clanging
  and cheering that carried right across
  the water.

  Teo stood in the wheelhouse door with
  his mouth open. "Did you plan this?"

  "No," Walt said, and found that he had
  to clear his throat twice to say it.

  Every landing was the same, and every
  crowd was bigger. At Church Steps the
  old minister rang the actual church
  bell, badly, out of time. At the
  school pier a whole class of children
  lined the rail in their yellow
  raincoats and shrieked the
  two-short-one-long signal back at them
  with their own voices. Some of them,
  Walt realized, had never ridden the
  [3mBluebell[0m in their lives. Their parents

